        situation: 'You are the GRO administrator. After a 15-year-old boy curses at a caregiver, the caregiver tells him he will be "kicked out" if it happens again, cuts off his phone calls with his mother for two weeks, and makes him dig a hole behind the building and fill it back in. The caregiver says nothing in the policy forbids any of this.',
        question: 'Which rules were violated, and what must you do?',
        discussion: 'Several. Forcing a child to do unproductive work is corporal punishment under §748.2303(b)(2)(C) and §748.2305 and is weighted High; moving dirt for no purpose is exactly the example given. Threatening loss of placement is prohibited discipline (§748.2307(3); §748.1101(b)(4)(A)(vi)). Cutting off contact with the child\'s mother as punishment is denying basic child rights as punishment, a High-weight prohibition (§748.2307(13)), and the right to make calls and keep family contact is protected by §748.1101(b)(2) and (3)(E). If the operation ever restricts contact, the reasons must be explained to the child and parent and documented, and a restriction lasting more than 30 days must be re-evaluated monthly by a professional level service provider (§748.1107, §748.1111). Also, any behavioral restriction lasting more than 14 days needs prior approval of the treatment director, service planning team, or a professional level service provider (§748.2309). Act now: restore the contact, retrain the caregiver, consider whether the incident must be reported under your abuse and neglect policies, and apply a logical, explained, individualized consequence under §748.2301.',
