        title: 'The Overdue Drill and the Locked Exit',
        situation: 'You are the GRO administrator. During a walk-through you learn that the last fire drill was eight months ago. The only designated exit from the boys\' cottage runs through the kitchen, and a deadbolt on the rear door requires a key to open from the inside. The evacuation diagram is on file in your office, not posted in the cottage.',
        question: 'List the violations and the corrective steps.',
        discussion: 'Fire drills must be unannounced and practiced at least once every six months from the last drill (§748.3239(a)), and severe weather drills must also occur every six months; eight months is overdue. Exit routes may not go through a kitchen or hazardous area, and may not use doors that need a key to open from the inside, unless the state or local fire inspector has approved in writing, signed and dated, with the approval kept at the operation (§748.3233(b)(4)(C), (E); §748.3237(c)). The diagram must be posted in a prominent location in every building used by children, employees, or volunteers (§748.3235; §748.191(3)), all Medium-High. Corrective steps: change to a deadbolt that opens from the inside without a key (or obtain the inspector\'s written approval), establish two distant, unblocked exits, post the diagram in the cottage, hold a drill with an alarm or smoke detector triggered, alternate exits used, children out within three minutes, and no elevators, then document date, time, type, and evacuation time (§748.3239(d)).',
