        situation: 'You are the GRO administrator. A 16-year-old girl has worked part-time at a grocery store for six months. Her paychecks have been deposited into the operation\'s general operating account, and the bookkeeper applies them toward her room and board. She was discharged 45 days ago and has not received her money. Her service plan says nothing about using her earnings for room and board.',
        question: 'What rules were violated, and what should you do?',
        discussion: 'A child has the right, depending on age and maturity, to seek employment, keep her own money, and have a bank account in her name (§748.1101(b)(3)(L)). The operation must account for a child\'s money separately from the operation\'s funds. It may not use a child\'s personal earnings, allowances, or gifts to pay room and board unless doing so is part of the service plan and the parent approves in writing, and it must give or send the child\'s money to the child, parent, or next placement within 30 days of discharge (§748.161(b), Medium-Low). Here the money was commingled, used without a plan provision or parent approval, and is now 15 days late. Immediately reconstruct the account, send the money to the child, parent, or next placement, document the transaction, change the bookkeeping procedure, and consider whether the service plan should address a savings or bank account as part of independent living preparation for a 16-year-old (§748.1337(b)(1)(G)).',
        keyTakeaway: 'A child\'s money is accounted for separately, may not pay room and board without a plan provision and written parent approval, and must go to the child, parent, or next placement within 30 days of discharge.',
